content this head did not write can no longer reconfigure the terminal

§3.1. **The exposure is real and it is this head's specifically, not a shared habit.**
leticl composes styled segments into a **cell grid** — `screen-put-string` writes only
cluster text and every SGR comes from the interned style table — so it *cannot* emit an
escape it did not author. This head composes ANSI-bearing strings and `paint_full`
writes each row **verbatim**, so an escape in model prose reached whatever terminal was
attached. The parity document's own summary of the two: *"B cannot emit an escape it
did not author. A can."*

**And it is not hypothetical.** Tool payloads were already sanitised (that is the fix
for the mouse-wheel bug), and §3.1 names the rest: model prose, reasoning, the user's
own message, the system row, fence bodies, diff excerpts read from disk. Every one of
those reached the glass raw.

## The bug I wrote while fixing it, which is why it gets its own function

`\n` **is a control character** — `char::is_control` is true of every C0 code,
including it. So the obvious move, passing model prose to the existing single-line
`without_control`, does not sanitise a document: it **collapses it onto one line**,
and every paragraph, list item and fenced block in every long message disappears. My
first cut of this did exactly that.

The mistake is invisible on the one-line fixture a hurried test would use and destroys
content on every real message, so it is a named function with its own doc rather than a
second `.map` at the call site:

    without_control_lines(s)   // per line, joined by the newline it split on
    without_control(line)      // unchanged: one line, controls become spaces

**A space, not a deletion**, which is the trade the payload path already made: dropping
a character silently reflows the line, and the wrapper about to measure it counts
columns.

## Everything sanitised, and where the seam is for each

| surface | seam | why there |
|---|---|---|
| model prose, live | the `Delta { Text }` arm | the answer enters here; `IncrementalMarkdown` is a frozen-prefix lexer (§13.3), so a control byte that reaches it is frozen into the stable half and cannot be removed later without re-lexing |
| model prose, settled | `TranscriptItem::Assistant` | before the lexer, so what is lexed and what is measured are one string |
| reasoning, live and settled | the same two | it renders through the markdown renderer as well |
| the operator's own paste | `TranscriptItem::User` | not distrust — a control byte copied out of a log arrives *as theirs*, and their own keystrokes cannot contain one (the decoder hands back `Key::Char`) |
| the system row | `TranscriptItem::System` | the bootstrap text is the harness's, not this head's |
| raw tool markup (`ctrl-x`) | `raw_call_lines` | accumulated for the whole turn and read by nothing else, so the one renderer is the right seam |
| a diff's two sides and its path | both `render_edit_view` call sites, **before the diff is taken** | a file's bytes are the one surface that is neither the model's nor the daemon's. Sanitising after the diff would leave a `\r` moving the cursor inside a row the renderer had already measured |
| a tool's failure reason | `outcome_why` | a command's stderr is arbitrary text |
| `decision_detail` | at each `format!` | three sentences there are somebody else's: the daemon's ask, the decider's basis, the guard's verdict and the operator's phrases it cites |
| notes, warned and decided | `note_lines_unfolded` | one renderer for both the transcript row and `/notes`, so one place covers both |
| a subagent's peeked scrollback | `subagent_out_lines` | another session's payloads and prose, read over a `Peek` and drawn here; sanitised at the builder so the spill file matches the pane |

That is the list §3.1 asked for, and the two that were **already** done are named for
completeness rather than credit: the settled tool payload and the job-output pane.

## The half neither of us had done: a test that would catch a regression

The requirement says leticl has two tests here and this head has none, and that is the
part worth reading as a criticism rather than as a to-do — **a guard nobody tests is
the state that let this sit.** So:

- `content_this_head_did_not_write_cannot_reconfigure_the_terminal` walks **every
  surface in the table above** with one sequence: `ESC ] 0 ; x BEL`, the window-title
  request, which is the one an operator can *watch happen*. It asserts the literal
  `ESC ] 0 ;` cannot occur in any rendered row, that no `BEL` survives, and — so the
  test cannot be satisfied by dropping the whole line — that the text is still there
  with the control byte spaced. The diff case is exercised through the real
  `render_edit_view`, so if the sanitisation were removed from either side the literal
  sequence appears and the test fails.
- `sanitising_a_document_keeps_its_lines_and_loses_its_controls` asserts the pair that
  is the whole function: the newline count is unchanged, and a sweep finds no control
  character other than `\n`. It also pins the single-line form's flattening behaviour,
  because "these two are not the same function" is the thing a later tidy-up would
  merge.

## What is deliberately **not** here

**The structural fix is a cell grid, and this is not that.** leticl cannot emit an
escape it did not author because it has no strings-with-escapes to emit; this commit
makes the same escapes impossible by sanitising the text that carries them. The parity
document's own caveat applies and is worth repeating: **leticl's immunity is incidental
to its painter, not a guard** — its own comment says so — and it would not survive a
change of painter. Neither would this survive a new renderer that forgets to call the
sanitiser, which is exactly why the test above is the durable half of it.

`cargo test -p letibot-tui`: **309 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored** (307 + 2).
`cargo test -p letibot-ui`: **101 passed, 0 failed**.

**Not verified, and said rather than glossed:** `rustfmt --check` cannot run — it writes
a temp file and `bash` is closed for that direction by the circuit breaker, which only
the operator lifts. One cargo at a time, as the last commit's note says; every run above
is one crate pair.

## Not in this commit

**`letibot-harnessd`, `-sessionlog`, `-tokencore` and `-turn` are still unrun since
`65725ce`.** Two attempts to run them contended on the cargo lock with my own builds and
sat at 0 % CPU; the honest statement is that I do not have their result rather than that
they fail. The only cross-crate dependency that commit could have had is a test built on
the removed refusal, and that lived in `clauses.rs`, which is green.

**R12 is blocked on a grant, not on a decision** — recorded with the exact query in
`~/Projects/blocked-overnight-2026-09-22.md`, because the store is outside this
session's view and every read of it opens a card that times out with nothing run.

